</style><style>.article .story-p{color:#d2d9cf}.radar-lab .label{font-size:11px}.details-callout{padding:24px;border-left:3px solid var(--lime);background:#20251d;margin:24px 0}.details-callout strong{color:var(--lime)}.article h2{scroll-margin-top:32px}.art-image figcaption{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted);margin-top:7px}</style></head><body><div class="shell"><header class="topbar"><a class="logo" href="/" aria-label="Anticipate homepage">ANTICIPATE<b>.</b></a><nav class="toplinks" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/#research">Research</a><a href="/series/nba-time-machine/">Time Machine</a><a href="#methodology">Method</a></nav></header>
<main><section class="hero" aria-labelledby="article-title">
<div><div class="episode"><b>07</b><span>NBA TIME MACHINE / AN ORIGINAL INVESTIGATION</span></div>
<h1 id="article-title">We tried to find the <em>next Magic Johnson.</em></h1>
<p class="dek">6-foot-9. Twelve assists a night. An entire offense at his fingertips. We searched 279 modern NBA players for Magic's closest match, and the answer raises a bigger question about what statistics can actually see.</p>
<div class="byline"><span>By <strong>{author}</strong></span><span>November 8, 2026</span><span>Research / 13 min read</span></div></div>
<figure class="hero-photo"><span class="photo-index">LOS ANGELES / NO. 32 / 1987</span><img src={heroImage} alt="Magic Johnson shooting a hook shot against the Boston Celtics in the 1987 NBA Finals" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
<figcaption>Magic Johnson's junior sky hook in the 1987 NBA Finals. Archival image via <a href="https://www.nba.com/watch/list/collection/best-clutch-moments" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NBA.com</a>; image rights remain with their original holders.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="stats-strip" aria-label="Magic Johnson 1986–87 season">
<div><strong>23.9</strong><small>Points / game</small></div><div><strong>12.2</strong><small>Assists / game</small></div><div><strong>6.3</strong><small>Rebounds / game</small></div><div><strong>65–17</strong><small>Lakers record</small></div><div><strong>MVP</strong><small>Regular season + Finals</small></div></section>
<div class="story-grid"><article class="article" aria-label="Magic Johnson statistical investigation">
{sections.slice(0,4).map(s=><section aria-labelledby={s.id}><h2 id={s.id}><span class="section-index">{s.n} / {s.tag}</span>{s.title}</h2>{s.paras.map((p,i)=><p class={i===0&&s.n==="01"?"lead":"story-p"} set:html={p}/>)}</section>)}
</article><aside class="toc" aria-label="Article navigation"><h3>Inside the investigation</h3>
{sections.map(s=><a href={"#"+s.id}>{s.n} — {s.title}</a>)}
<a href="#radar">05 — Interactive comparison</a><a href="#physical-radar">05 — Physical comparison</a><a href="#sources">Sources and research</a>
<div class="recirc">NEXT IN THE SERIES<b>Shaquille O'Neal</b>Episode 08 / 1999–00 / Coming soon</div>
</aside></div>
<section class="fullbleed" aria-label="Magic Johnson modern model matches">
<div class="eyebrow">THE RESULT / 279 QUALIFIED PLAYERS</div><h2>Five possible matches. Five different answers.</h2>
<p class="intro">The ranking measures seven production statistics, two body measurements and a positional category. A smaller distance means a closer measured season profile, not an equivalence in talent or greatness.</p>
<div class="ranking-list">{matches.map((p,i)=><div class="rank-row"><div class="rankno">{String(i+1).padStart(2,"0")}</div><div><h3>{p.name}</h3><p>{p.team.toUpperCase()} / 2025–26</p></div><div class="distance">{p.dist.toFixed(4)}</div></div>)}</div>
<p class="smallnote" style="margin-top:14px">Distances use Anticipate's standard model: 70% statistics, 20% physical measurements and 10% broad position. The values are distances, not percentages.</p>
</section>
<div class="story-grid"><article class="article">
<h2 id="radar"><span class="section-index">05 / INTERACTIVE MATCHUP</span>What does Magic actually share with these five?</h2>
<p>Select a current player to compare seven important season-level rates with Magic's 1986–87 profile. The statistical radar keeps every axis fixed when switching players. Below it, a linked profile radar compares height, weight and broad playing position. Exact values remain visible so you can see the trade-offs behind both shapes.</p>
<div class="radar-lab"><div class="eyebrow">ANTICIPATE / THE COMPARISON LAB</div><h3>One historic guard. Five modern possibilities.</h3><p>Lime represents Magic Johnson. Teal represents the chosen modern player.</p>
<div class="compare-buttons" aria-label="Select comparison">{matches.map((p,i)=><button type="button" data-player-index={i} aria-pressed={i===0?"true":"false"}>{p.name}</button>)}</div>
<div class="radar-inner"><div><svg class="radar-svg" viewBox="0 0 480 440" role="img" aria-label="Radar comparing Magic Johnson with James Harden">
{[.25,.5,.75,1].map(f=><polygon class="ring" points={ring(f)}/>)}
{axes.map((a,i)=>{const q=point(i,axes.length,132);return <line class="spoke" x1="240" y1="211" x2={q.x} y2={q.y}/>;})}
<polygon class="moses-shape" points={polygon(magic)}/><polygon class="peer-shape" id="peer-polygon" points={polygon(matches[0])}/>
{axes.map((a,i)=>{const q=point(i,axes.length,174);return <text class="label" x={q.x} y={q.y}>{a.label}</text>;})}</svg>
<div class="legend"><span><i></i>Magic (1986–87)</span><span><i class="teal"></i><span id="legend-name">James Harden</span> (2025–26)</span></div></div>
<div class="radar-side" aria-live="polite"><div class="eyebrow">SELECTED PLAYER</div><h4 id="peer-name">James Harden</h4><p id="peer-description">{matches[0].description}</p>
<dl class="value-list" id="peer-values">{axes.map(a=><div><dt>{a.label}</dt><dd><strong>{magic[a.key].toFixed(1)}</strong> / {matches[0][a.key].toFixed(1)}</dd></div>)}</dl>
<p class="note">Each pair reads Magic / selected player. rTS is true-shooting percentage points above or below the player's league average. The full model includes height, weight and broad position.</p>
</div></div></div>

<div class="radar-lab physical-lab" id="physical-radar">
  <div class="eyebrow">ANTICIPATE / PHYSICAL COMPARISON</div>
  <h3>Height, weight and position.</h3>
  <p>The player selected above also appears here. This three-axis radar compares listed height and weight alongside the model’s broad position category (Guard, Forward or Center).</p>
  <div class="radar-inner">
    <div>
      <svg class="radar-svg" id="physical-radar-svg" viewBox="0 0 480 440" role="img" aria-label="Physical radar comparing Magic Johnson with James Harden">
        {[.25,.5,.75,1].map(f=><polygon class="ring" points={physicalRing(f)}/>)}
        {physicalAxes.map((a,i)=>{const q=point(i,physicalAxes.length,132);return <line class="spoke" x1="240" y1="211" x2={q.x} y2={q.y}/>;})}
        <polygon class="moses-shape" points={physicalPolygon(magic)}/>
        <polygon class="peer-shape" id="physical-peer-polygon" points={physicalPolygon(matches[0])}/>
        {physicalAxes.map((a,i)=>{const q=point(i,physicalAxes.length,174);return <text class="label" x={q.x} y={q.y}>{a.label}</text>;})}
      </svg>
      <div class="legend"><span><i></i>Magic (1986–87)</span><span><i class="teal"></i><span id="physical-legend-name">James Harden</span> (2025–26)</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="radar-side" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="eyebrow">PHYSICAL &amp; POSITION PROFILE</div>
      <h4 id="physical-peer-name">James Harden</h4>
      <dl class="value-list" id="physical-values">
        <div><dt>HEIGHT</dt><dd><strong>{magic.height} cm</strong> / {matches[0].height} cm</dd></div>
        <div><dt>WEIGHT</dt><dd><strong>{magic.weight} lb</strong> / {matches[0].weight} lb</dd></div>
        <div><dt>POSITION</dt><dd><strong>{magic.position}</strong> / {matches[0].position} (match)</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="note">Each pair reads Magic / selected player. Height and weight contribute 20% of the model distance; the separate broad position-match penalty contributes 10%.</p>
      <p class="physical-note">Three distinct axes are shown. Fixed display scales: height 175–220 cm; weight 170–270 lb. Position is categorical: 100% = same group as Magic (Guard), 0% = different group. This is not a numerical measure of positional ability. The chart does not measure wingspan, speed, strength or athleticism.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="details-callout"><strong>What this cannot show:</strong> transition timing, passing creativity, speed of decision-making, pace and the different shot diets of 1987 and 2026. The distance describes measured production, not identical basketball.</div>
{sections.slice(4).map(s=><section aria-labelledby={s.id}><h2 id={s.id}><span class="section-index">{s.n} / {s.tag}</span>{s.title}</h2>{s.paras.map(p=><p class="story-p" set:html={p}/>)}</section>)}
<h2 id="sources"><span class="section-index">SOURCES / METHODOLOGY</span>Where the numbers come from</h2>
<p>Historic season results were cross-checked against the Lakers, NBA and Basketball-Reference records. The 279 eligible modern players, historical reference row, feature weights and ranked distances come from Anticipate's public NBA Time Machine dataset. The underlying methodology notes that its historical advanced-rate rows were supplied by the researcher and have not been fully source-audited across the project. This article should be understood as an editorial statistical comparison rather than a validated measure of playing style or greatness.</p>
<ul class="source-list">{sources.map(s=><li><div><a href={s[1]} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{s[0]}</a><small>{s[2]}</small></div></li>)}</ul>
</article><aside></aside></div>
<section class="endcap"><div class="eyebrow">NBA TIME MACHINE / EPISODE 07</div><h2>The model has an answer. The film asks a different question.</h2><p>Magic Johnson changed the speed of an entire game. Which current player feels closest to that kind of control?</p><div class="action-row"><a class="cta" href="/series/nba-time-machine/">All episodes</a><a class="cta secondary" href="/research/hakeem-olajuwon-time-machine/">Previous: Hakeem Olajuwon</a><a class="cta secondary" href="https://github.com/mudia-OE/anticipate-nba-time-machine" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Model and data</a></div></section>
